WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:10.500
In the late 1950’s and 1960’s people began
to experiment with the idea of computer to

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.710
computer communication….

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:17.120
all information, no matter the form, is stored
in the computer’s memory as a sequences

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.690
of bits.

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:24.280
To send these bits they created hardware which
could transmit a clocked sequence of electrical

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.330
pulses along a wire.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:30.780
On the receiving end the computer would capture,
store and convert the bits into the information

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.489
they represent.

00:00:32.489 --> 00:00:36.739
Doing this with two computers, along a single
line, is quite simple.

00:00:36.739 --> 00:00:40.180
But of course, other people wanted to connect
their computers together, too.

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:42.170
Leading to a computer network.

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:48.570
One simple solution to make this network would
be to connect all machines to all other machines.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:53.430
but as you add more and more computers this
requires an impractical amount of line…plus

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.380
it’s incredibly wasteful.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:59.630
Telephone networks at the time took a more
practical approach, individuals were connected

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:05.149
to a Hub (such as a city switchboard) which
was a point through which many people could

00:01:05.149 --> 00:01:06.460
connect.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:11.200
At this hub would be an operator with a switchboard
would manually patch people together by closing

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.590
a circuit between them (known as a circuit
switched network).

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:20.859
So if PERSON A wants to connect to PERSON
B, they both get patched onto the long distance

00:01:20.859 --> 00:01:23.299
line through the hub.

00:01:23.299 --> 00:01:27.450
the drawback of this method is nobody else
in the network can use the long distance line

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:30.719
until they finish their conversation.

00:01:30.719 --> 00:01:33.869
We call this line the bottleneck of the network.

00:01:33.869 --> 00:01:40.200
By 1972 the designers of what we now call
the internet were building ARPANET.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.469
It began as a small network of computers at
universities on both sides of the country

00:01:44.469 --> 00:01:46.229
connected together.

00:01:46.229 --> 00:01:50.249
So they came up with a clever way of weaving
computer conversations together.

00:01:50.249 --> 00:01:58.030
by breaking up all digital messages into chunks
known as packets.

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:02.889
The job of the hub is simple: Send packets
as they arrive from various sources on a first

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:05.049
come, first served basis.

00:02:05.049 --> 00:02:09.149
each packet labeled by its source and destination

00:02:09.149 --> 00:02:13.620
On the receiving end this weaving is undone
and the packets are put back into their original

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:14.620
sequence.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:20.000
Effectively squeezing multiple conversations
along the line at the same time...

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.360
New cities were connected to hubs and those
hubs were connected to each other.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:30.500
So more and more digital data needed to get
squeezed down these long distance lines.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:36.560
But there is a fundamental limit to how many
bits you can squeeze down any line per second.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.840
This limit is known as the line capacity (or
Bandwidth).

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:45.390
And as you add more and more and more households
to the network, then you’ll run into a problem

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:48.620
we’ve all experienced in traffic….a waiting
line develops

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:50.680
(waiting line visual)

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:57.120
If you are watching a streaming video you
will notice these missing packets as unwanted

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.850
pauses in your stream.

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:04.470
To see exactly how this happens, let’s do
a simple example and ignore all the people

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:07.260
on the internet except one household.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:12.620
Imagine this household is watching a video
stream from Washington upstairs and downstairs

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:16.250
someone is watching an audio stream from California.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:21.600
The video packet could take THIS pathway and
at the same time the audio packet could take

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:22.720
THIS pathway.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:24.440
No problem.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:27.790
But now let’s add another household to the
network, in a separate city.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:31.710
And assume they also want the identical audio
and video stream.

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:34.150
Perhaps they are popular shows.

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:38.790
They have direct a pathway to the music source,
but they also need the middle path for their

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:40.670
video source.

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:45.860
This causes a waiting line, or queue, to develop,
so the hub will transmit two packets down

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:47.970
the line, one after the other.

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:52.900
This is what we mean when we say the network
is ‘slowing down’ - people begin to wait

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:56.430
for their packets due to queues which develop
at the hub.

00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:02.590
so The BIG engineering problem today becomes,
how can we make these queues at our bottlenecks

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:04.370
smaller?

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:09.890
In the year 2000 a seminal paper was published
(Ahlswede et al) and it’s based on a clever

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:14.070
strategy for what happens at the nodes in
a network...

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:18.370
Instead of treating packets like cars, and
sending them one at a time.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:23.259
The idea was to treat packets as numbers.

00:04:23.259 --> 00:04:27.980
And blend them together using mathematical
operations, such as addition.

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:32.889
Resulting in a new packet which contains a
mixture of information from more than one

00:04:32.889 --> 00:04:33.909
packets.

00:04:33.909 --> 00:04:40.050
Think of these mixed packets as providing
clues about what’s in them

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:45.100
Let’s return to our example to see exactly
how it works...

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:49.210
Let’s say the video packet being sent can
be represented by the number 1.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:52.580
And the audio packet can be represented by
the number 4.

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:56.729
When the video packet hits the first hub,
it’s the only one to arrive so it’s sent

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:59.582
unchanged along all the output lines.

00:04:59.582 --> 00:05:03.639
Same thing happens when the music packet when
it hits the first hub.

00:05:03.639 --> 00:05:10.020
But now look at the middle up, it receives
2 packets, and this time it adds them together

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:14.509
and sends the result as a single packet.

00:05:14.509 --> 00:05:18.639
Notice now, both households receive their
packets just as fast for each household as

00:05:18.639 --> 00:05:22.580
if they were alone on the network!

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:27.759
Finally the key step is to look at how we
can unmix these mixtures on the receiving

00:05:27.759 --> 00:05:28.759
end.

00:05:28.759 --> 00:05:30.620
Let’s look at Household 1 first.

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:33.360
It receives the video packet directly.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:39.331
to extract the music packet out of the mixture
it needs to SOLVE for the missing value, by

00:05:39.331 --> 00:05:42.509
doing simple math we all learn in school.

00:05:42.509 --> 00:05:43.979
We know the Video packet = 1

00:05:43.979 --> 00:05:47.349
And the Video + Music packet = 5

00:05:47.349 --> 00:05:48.349
Therefore,

00:05:48.349 --> 00:05:52.039
The Music packet equals 4.

00:05:52.039 --> 00:05:54.740
And that’s the great insight of this paper.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:06:01.430
We treat each mixture as an equation, the
receiving computer solves for the variables,

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:05.300
or packets, it needs.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:10.560
This is in sharp contrast to how the internet
was originally designed.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:16.120
Because it allow smultiple packets to travel
the same line at the same time, instead of

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:19.659
having to get each packet in line and wait
its turn

00:06:19.659 --> 00:06:25.090
And this system practically works because
computers can now do basic arithmetic operations

00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:30.009
needed to solve these linear equations incredibly
fast, millions of times per second

00:06:30.009 --> 00:06:39.240
By 2020 it is expected that ~75% of network
traffic will be video streams and the traffic

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:44.719
from mobile phones alone will be 30 exabytes.

00:06:44.719 --> 00:06:48.909
And we increasingly rely on our communication
networks for services that are considered

00:06:48.909 --> 00:06:52.680
fundamental such as health, finance, education...

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:57.469
Therefore solving problems related to network
congestion is critical.

00:06:57.469 --> 00:07:02.939
And network coding is a great approach because
it allows us to mix information packets instead

00:07:02.939 --> 00:07:07.509
of having to get each packet in line and wait
its turn.

00:07:07.509 --> 00:07:12.139
This video presented the basic principle of
network coding in a simplified manner, but

00:07:12.139 --> 00:07:17.219
there still exist many open questions, as
well as new applications to explore.

00:07:17.219 --> 00:07:21.729
Such as wireless networks, security, distributed
computing and storage.

00:07:21.729 --> 00:07:26.529
An active research area in information theory
today is how to best leverage this paradigm

00:07:26.529 --> 00:07:29.610
shift to network coding as we build our digital
future.

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:29.870
END

